---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import DedupeLinesTool from '../../components/tools/DedupeLinesTool.astro';
import { toolBySlug } from '../../data/tools';
import { dedupeLines, SAMPLE_INPUT } from '../../lib/dedupe-lines';

const tool = toolBySlug('remove-duplicate-lines');

// Every example below is produced by the real engine at build time.
const sampleIn = SAMPLE_INPUT; // the same text the Load sample button inserts
const caseSensitive = dedupeLines(sampleIn);
const caseInsensitive = dedupeLines(sampleIn, { ignoreCase: true });
const keepLast = dedupeLines('a\nb\na\nc', { keep: 'last' }).output.replace(/\n/g, ', ');
const onlyDup = dedupeLines('a\nb\na\nc\nc', { onlyDuplicates: true }).output.replace(/\n/g, ', ');
const noTrim = dedupeLines('a\n a \na', { trim: false });

const steps = [
  'Paste the list, one entry per line.',
  'Tick <strong>Ignore letter case</strong> if <code>Apple</code> and <code>apple</code> should count as the same line; leave <strong>Trim spaces before comparing</strong> on so stray spaces do not create false uniques.',
  'Choose which copy to keep, <em>First occurrence</em> or <em>Last occurrence</em>, and whether to sort the result.',
  'Read the counter: it shows how many unique lines remain and how many were removed.',
  '<strong>Copy to clipboard</strong> or <strong>Download .txt</strong>.',
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is this the same as “sort unique” on the command line?',
    a: 'It does the same job as <code>sort -u</code>, but keeps your original order by default instead of always sorting, and adds options Unix tools don’t: ignore-case matching, trimming before comparing, and choosing whether the first or last copy of a repeated line survives.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does “Keep only lines that had duplicates” do?',
    a: 'Instead of removing duplicates, it flips the filter around and shows you only the lines that appeared more than once (one copy of each), which is useful for spotting exactly what was duplicated in a large export.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does trimming change the lines in the output, or just the comparison?',
    a: 'Both: when “Trim spaces before comparing” is on, the output lines are trimmed too, so “apple” and “ apple ” are treated as the same line, and the surviving copy has no stray spaces.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How large a list can it handle?',
    a: 'Comfortably into the hundreds of thousands of lines: 200,000 lines dedupe in under a tenth of a second in testing. The only limit is your browser’s memory for the text itself, and typing is debounced so editing a big paste stays responsive.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout {tool} {steps} {faq}>
  <DedupeLinesTool />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>When duplicate lines sneak in</h2>
    <p>
      Merged spreadsheets, combined mailing lists, log files tailed twice, and copy-pasted data from multiple sources all tend
      to accumulate exact repeats. A quick visual scan rarely catches every one, especially once a list runs past a screen's
      worth of lines. This tool compares every line against every other line and keeps just one copy of each.
    </p>
    <p>
      By default it preserves your original ordering (the first time a line appears it stays where it was, rather than the
      whole list being resorted), so a deduplicated list is a strict subset of what you pasted in, easy to diff against the
      original if you need to check what was removed. If the values are on one line separated by commas rather than one per
      line, split them first with the <a href="/tools/comma-separated-list-to-column">list to column</a> converter.
    </p>

    <h2>Example</h2>
    <p>Input:</p>
    <pre><code>{sampleIn}</code></pre>
    <p>With the default options, <code>Apple</code> and <code>apple</code> are different lines, so the result is:</p>
    <pre><code>{caseSensitive.output}</code></pre>
    <p>With <em>Ignore letter case</em> on, <code>Apple</code> is treated as a duplicate of <code>apple</code> and dropped:</p>
    <pre><code>{caseInsensitive.output}</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">The counter reads “{caseInsensitive.unique} lines · {caseInsensitive.removed} removed” for the second case, {caseSensitive.unique} lines · {caseSensitive.removed} removed for the first.</p>

    <h2>Options explained</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Ignore letter case</strong> compares lowercased copies of each line. The surviving line keeps its original casing; whichever copy came first (or last) wins.</li>
      <li><strong>Trim spaces before comparing</strong> removes leading and trailing spaces and tabs from every line, in the output as well as for the comparison.</li>
      <li><strong>Remove blank lines</strong> drops empty lines entirely. Untick it and blank lines are treated like any other line: they are all duplicates of each other, so exactly one survives.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep only lines that had duplicates</strong> inverts the filter: the output is one copy of each line that appeared two or more times, and nothing else. <code>a, b, a, c, c</code> gives <code>{onlyDup}</code>; if nothing was repeated, the result is empty.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep: First occurrence / Last occurrence</strong> chooses which copy survives. With <em>Last</em>, the output order follows the last occurrences: <code>a, b, a, c</code> becomes <code>{keepLast}</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Sort result: Keep original order / A → Z / Z → A</strong> sorts the deduplicated output in natural, case-insensitive order (<code>x2</code> before <code>x10</code>, <code>A</code> before <code>b</code>).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Edge cases and common errors</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Only exact text matches.</strong> <code>café</code> typed with a precomposed é and <code>café</code> typed with a combining accent look identical but are different strings, and both survive. Normalise upstream if that matters, for example with <code>unicodedata.normalize("NFC", s)</code> in Python.</li>
      <li><strong>Trailing spaces with trimming off</strong> make <code>a</code> and <code>a </code> different lines: <code>a, [space]a[space], a</code> gives {noTrim.unique} unique lines instead of 1.</li>
      <li><strong>Windows (<code>\r\n</code>) and old-Mac (<code>\r</code>) line endings</strong> are recognised; the output always uses <code>\n</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Blank lines are not counted as “removed”.</strong> The output counter compares non-blank lines only, so five pasted lines with two blanks reads “3 lines” with nothing removed. The input counter shows the raw line total if you need to reconcile.</li>
      <li><strong>Empty input</strong> gives an empty output and a count of 0. No error is raised.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Do the same thing on the command line</h2>
    <pre><code>sort -u file.txt                        # sorted, unique lines
awk '!seen[$0]++' file.txt              # unique lines, original order preserved
Get-Content file.txt | Select-Object -Unique   # PowerShell, original order</code></pre>
    <p class="muted"><code>sort -u</code> and <code>awk</code> are case-sensitive and do not trim; add <code>-f</code> to <code>sort</code> for case-folding.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
